A||||4||Logicdragon||Computer Questions||||1||
Z||000000||Logicdragon||08-18-2001||04:02 PM||||I read an article a month or so ago in a sport compact car mag that talked about hooking up a computer (laptop/desktop) to gm cars and diagnosing engine codes.  It mentioned the software used but I forgot the name of it and I don't have the magazine very accessible right now...its about 12,000 miles away.  I think it was like something-com diagnostics or similar to that.  Anyone know what it is?<P>Logic Dragon     ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mach10||08-18-2001||04:29 PM||||That setup is ODB-II compliant. For the ODB-I (ALDL or C3) system on the fiero, something a little older must be used. Alternatively, you can build your own setup, and plug it into some custom software. There's a very talented individual that has done this. His page is right here: <BR> <A HREF="http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/techno.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/techno.htm</A> <P>The appropriate stuff is at the bottom of the page. I've done it, and it's really cool and easy to build. Still have to buy his advanced software. Maybe when I have more money...||||reg||7
Z||000002||00lE||08-18-2001||06:36 PM||||Diacom software is probably what you are referring to:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.rinda.com/auto/auto.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rinda.com/auto/auto.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||08-18-2001||07:06 PM||||All for the paltry sum of $299USD.<P>I have mine built. Cost me $15 in electronics parts, and the ALDL software from the link I posted costs $15.00.<BR>Admittedly, the DIACOM stuff will work with ANY pre-92 GM, but I was interested only in my car. Oh, and I think that the ALDL software will not work with DIS. I'd have to check on that. Again, it's not applicable in my case.<P>*EDIT*<BR>My mistake. It should work with DIS. I was thinking of the ScanTool by someone else...<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-18-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000004||theogre||08-18-2001||07:20 PM||||Diacom is the main one used on a PC.<P>I beleive, but am not 100% sure, that the DIS 1227748 ECM uses a later revision of the communications interface. It is not the same as the interface used for all other Fiero.<P>You can get software and stuff that will talk to both OBD1 versions. My AutoXray talks to both GM OBD1 systems and cost about $150-200 depending where you get it.<P>Nice thing about AutoXray is that you don't need a lap top or risk damaging on you have by droping it or whatever while you work. Bad thing is that while it captures everything, you have to do some shufling around to view the data. I've had one for a couple years now and that's only ever been a problem 1 time when I was trying to do some testing of the fan controls.||||reg||1
